(FIXME: CVE number assignment)
git-annex was vulnerable to a private data exfiltration attack. Joey Hess
discovered this attack.

This security hole was fixed in git-annex (FIXME: version)

## attack descriptions

To perform these attacks, the attacker needs to have control over one of
the remotes of the victim's git-annex repository. For example, they may
provide a git-annex repository that the victim clones.

The attacker also needs to have information about another git-annex 
repository owned by the victim. This could be a published git repository,
with annexed file contents that the victim is keeping private.

In the simplest form of the attack, the attacker provides a git-annex
repository that the victim clones. When git-annex is initialized in the
victim's clone, it autoenables a special remote that the attacker has
configured to access the victim's other, private git-annex repository.
When the victim runs a command such as `git-annex sync --content`,
their private files are retrieved from the special remote and sent to the
attacker's git-annex repository.

In another form of the attack, autoenable of special remotes is not needed.
Instead, the attacker provides a special remote (eg a S3 bucket) along with
their git-annex repository. The attacker waits for the victim to clone the
repository and manually enable and use their special remote. Then they update
the configuration of the special remote, retargeting it to access the victim's
own special remote (eg the victim's private S3 bucket). When the victim runs a
command such as `git-annex sync --content`, their private files are retrieved
from their special remote and sent to the attacker's git-annex repository.

## git-annex security fixes

To avoid these attacks, after a new special remote is enabled, git-annex
will refuse to send annexed files to all remotes, and will also block git
pushes, until the user verifies that the configuration of the special
remote is safe.

Additionally, changes to the configuration of already enabled 
special remotes, that are received via the git-annex branch,
will not take effect until the user has verified they are safe.

After upgrading git-annex, existing special remotes will continue to work
as before. If a special remote could have been configured by a third party,
you should verify its configuration using the `git-annex verifyremote`
command.
